In article <allenk.684027295@bullyrag> allenk@bullyrag.ugcs.caltech.edu (Allen Knutson) writes:
                             [...]
>But I don't think this is the way to fix it. Why would one find GRAY
>dragons particularly? (Yes, I see why it's necessary for this process,
>but it doesn't make game sense.)

Sure it does.  Because gray dragons are the magic dragons.  You know, eat a
black dragon, gain intrinsic disintegration resistance; polymorph a black
dragon, gain a disintegration resistant suit of armor (that confers
disintegration resistance).  Likewise for each colored dragon and its
corresponding intrinsic.

Now, if you polymorph a gray dragon corpse, then you get a magic-resistant
suit of armor.  But there is no intrinsic magic resistance (thank goodness -
that could ruin your game).  So, since dragons are the great source of
intrinsics (other creatures give intrinsics, but dragons are a dependable and
general-purpose source of intrinsics), it makes sense to use the gray dragon
corpse for a magic-related intrinsic.

I think that in the interests of keeping the game consistent that all
characters should have a *chance* of gaining an intrinsic spell by eating a
gray dragon corpse, but it should be a much smaller chance unless the
character is a wizard.

I still think that this is a very sensible approach to making magic more
important (right now I play wizards but rarely use the spells I learn).
Spells would continue to work the same way they always have, but there would
be a mechanism for making them more useful, with some effort on the player's
part.

I can really see this as making a spellbook into a highly coveted object for a
wizard to search for.  Yet it does not make spellbooks automatically
overpowering, in and of themselves.

If you don't think this makes sense, I would like to see your argument against
this approach, in terms of direct and specific objections.  I would also like
to see the comments of anyone who thinks this is a good approach to improving
magic-use.
